# Root build context filter, and since 2026-08-28 the only one CI applies.
#
# The <dockerfile>.dockerignore convention is a BuildKit feature and nothing
# else implements it. On a developer machine `docker build` is BuildKit (the
# default since Docker 23), so the four projects/<member>/Dockerfile.dockerignore
# files win there and this file is never consulted. GitLab CI builds with
# kaniko, because the shared runners can run neither docker-in-docker nor
# buildah (see .gitlab-ci.yml), and kaniko reads only the root .dockerignore.
#
# So the two build paths filter the context differently, and this is the file
# that decides what a *published* image was built from. Without it CI would
# upload .git, docs/ and data/raw/ as build context on every build -- and on a
# developer machine falling back to a non-BuildKit builder, a 2.27 GB .venv.
#
# Contents are the union of the four per-Dockerfile files, which is safe for all
# of them: it deliberately does NOT exclude data/scraper/registry/ or
# data/scraper/specs/, which projects/scraper/Dockerfile COPYs and spec_engine
# reads at runtime. Keep it that way -- excluding them produces an image that
# builds, starts, and then finds nothing to scrape.
.git
.github
.gitignore
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
__pycache__
*.pyc
*.secret
data/raw/
data/publications.jsonl
data/state.json
# Test suites, at the root and in every member. Before the workspace split a
# bare `tests/` covered everything; a dockerignore pattern is anchored at the
# build context, so it no longer reaches projects/*/tests or libs/*/tests.
tests/
projects/*/tests/
libs/*/tests/
docs/
notebooks/

venv/
.venv/

# Scraper runtime output. registry/ and specs/ ARE copied in (see the Dockerfile);
# these three are machine-written and would only bloat the build context.
data/scraper/cache/
data/scraper/var/
data/scraper/output/
